faqs:
  - question: What is a normal blood pressure?
    answer: >-
      A healthy blood pressure is usually between 90/60 and 120/80. High
      blood pressure is usually 140/90 or higher when measured at a surgery
      or pharmacy, or 135/85 or higher when measured at home.
  - question: Can high blood pressure be cured?
    answer: >-
      For most people it is a long-term condition that is controlled rather
      than cured. Healthy changes and medicines can bring it down to a safe
      level, and some people are able to reduce or stop medicines over time
      with their doctor's advice.
  - question: Where can I get my blood pressure checked?
    answer: >-
      At your GP surgery, at many pharmacies, or at home with a monitor. In
      England, many pharmacies offer free checks to adults aged 40 or over,
      and the free NHS Health Check for people aged 40 to 74 includes a
      blood pressure check.
---

**High blood pressure**, also called **hypertension**, means your blood is pushing against the walls of your blood vessels harder than it should. Over time this strains your heart, blood vessels, kidneys and eyes.

It is very common, especially as people get older. It rarely causes symptoms, which is why it is sometimes called a "silent" condition. The only way to know is to **have it checked**.

## What the numbers mean

A blood pressure reading has two numbers, for example **120/80**:

- the **top number** is the pressure when your heart pushes blood out
- the **bottom number** is the pressure when your heart rests between beats

| Reading | What it usually means |
| --- | --- |
| 90/60 to 120/80 | Healthy |
| 120/80 to 140/90 | Higher than ideal, so healthy changes can help |
| 140/90 or higher (135/85 or higher at home) | High blood pressure |

## Why it matters

Untreated high blood pressure raises your risk of:

- heart attacks and heart failure
- strokes
- kidney disease
- vascular dementia
- sight problems

The good news is that **lowering your blood pressure lowers these risks**.

## What causes it?

Often there is no single cause. Things that raise your risk include:

- being **older**
- eating **too much salt**
- being **overweight** or not active enough
- drinking **a lot of alcohol**
- **smoking**
- a **family history** of high blood pressure
- being of **Black African or Black Caribbean** heritage
- some conditions, such as **kidney disease** or **diabetes**

## Getting diagnosed

If a check shows high blood pressure, your GP will usually ask you to check it over a few days, either with a **home monitor** or a small **24 hour monitor** you wear. This makes sure the reading was not just high because you were nervous at the surgery. You may also have blood and urine tests and a heart trace (ECG).

## Treatment

Healthy changes help everyone. Many people also need one or more medicines. See [high blood pressure treatment](/conditions/physical/high-blood-pressure/treatment/).

{% callout type="danger" title="Call 999 if" %}
Your blood pressure is **180/120 or higher** and you have symptoms such as a severe headache, chest pain, breathlessness, blurred vision, confusion, or weakness on one side of the body. These can be signs of a medical emergency.
{% /callout %}
